  "text": "MSdocTr-Lite: A Lite Transformer for Full Page Multi-script Handwriting\nRecognition\nMarwa Dhiafa,b,c,*, Ahmed Cheikh Rouhoua, Yousri Kessentinib,c, Sinda Ben Salema\naInstaDeep\nb Digital Research Center of Sfax, Sfax 3021, Tunisia\ncSM@RTS : Laboratory of Signals, systeMs, aRtificial Intelligence and neTworkS\nAbstract\nThe Transformer has quickly become the dominant architecture for various pattern recognition tasks due to its capacity\nfor long-range representation. However, transformers are data-hungry models and need large datasets for training. In\nHandwritten Text Recognition (HTR), collecting a massive amount of labeled data is a complicated and expensive task.\n2\nIn this paper, we propose a lite transformer architecture for full-page multi-script handwriting recognition. The proposed\n4ar\nmodel comes with three advantages: First, to solve the common problem of data scarcity, we propose a lite transformer\nmodel that can be trained on a reasonable amount of data, which is the case of most HTR public datasets, without the\nneed for external data. Second, it can learn the reading order at page-level thanks to a curriculum learning strategy,\nallowing it to avoid line segmentation errors, exploit a larger context and reduce the need for costly segmentation\nannotations. Third, it can be easily adapted to other scripts by applying a simple transfer-learning process using only\npage-level labeled images. Extensive experiments on different datasets with different scripts (French, English, Spanish,\nand Arabic) show the effectiveness of the proposed model.\nKeywords: Seq2Seq model, page-level recognition, Handwritten Text Recognition, Multi-script, Transformer, Transfer\nLearning\n1. Introduction\nhave been introduced, where word images are extracted\nHandwritten Text Recognition (HTR) aims to transform\nfrom the document before feeding them into an HTR sys-\nscanned handwritten documents into machine-encoded\ntem. But, this kind of approach faced so